p = "experiments/E342_pair-head-host-time/experiment.md"
s = open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()
def rep(a, b):
    global s
    assert s.count(a) == 1, a[:70]
    s = s.replace(a, b)
rep("## 1. Part A (CPU, measured 2026-11-14 16:10; the code is in the commit of this card): `jgt._groups` vectorised", "## 1. Part A (CPU, measured 2026-11-14 16:10, installed by commit 27fee60b at 21:48 after two critic passes of the patch): `jgt._groups` vectorised")
rep("**Labels: 0 mismatches on 3 300 real sets and 20 000 random clustered sets** (float32; the selftest in `jgt.py` adds 1 500 sets in float32 and float64 against the loop kept as the oracle).", "**Labels (the critic's run on the installed text):** 0 mismatches on 3 322 non-empty real sets of the E312 VAL dumps (3 300 with n >= 2 and 22 with n = 1; the dump holds 3 492 sets, 170 with n = 0), `JEnds.rank_matrix` bitwise equal on 1 746 frames, 0 mismatches on 20 000 random clustered float32 sets and on 3 304 real float64 GT sets of the loader path, and on an adversarial suite (exact-boundary grids, degenerate rows, chains and shuffled paths up to 32 ends, f32 and f64). **Limit of 'bit for bit':** in float64 the labels are identical on every tested input, but the arithmetic is not bit-identical at inputs that sit exactly on a threshold (OpenBLAS ddot uses FMA: 1 ulp differences in the dot product and the norm; on 40 000 constructed pairs at exactly 25.0 degrees +-3 ulp, 498 / 529 differ in the angle test; real data sits there with a probability of about 1e-15); the old float64 result is itself CPU- and BLAS-dependent at that knife edge. The selftest in `jgt.py` compares with the loop kept as the oracle and pins the edge semantics (boundary `<=`, a zero heading, float32 headings with self-dot above 1, a shuffled path of 32 ends): it is an equivalence test, so it passes with the old code too. **Speed by set size** (the critic's loaded box, old -> new per set): 2 ends 15 -> 53 microseconds (slower), 3 ends 56 -> 79, 4 ends 89 -> 61, 13 ends 1 488 -> 88, 32 ends 10 700 -> 141; groups 1.17 -> 0.105 ms, groups plus ranks 1.91 -> 0.63 ms, one `rank_matrix` call 5.5 -> 2.6 ms per frame.")
rep("Code: `training/grid/jgt.py` (`_groups`, selftest), README row; no other file.", "Code: `training/grid/jgt.py` (`_groups`, selftest), README row; no other file (committed 27fee60b).\n**Mixed versions (stated, as the critic asked):** the E317.g_s1 training and the evaluation jobs that were running at 21:48 keep the old code in memory; later chain stages (post_pair, E318.u, E319, the reads) and every run that starts after 21:48 use the new one; the outputs are identical (above), and each run's `lcr/<id>` tag shows the code version it started from. The 17.09 ms of `temp/jr/timing_junction.json` (2026-11-13) predates the patch: the first idle-GPU timing after the install is the new baseline, expected about 15.7 ms, and the two are not comparable across the install.")
